{"author":{"id":"c7109691837125ce179ae67c00dda2acb755e6dfb3646ed3e9aad234d639bbcc"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1775652706,"nonce":"58b+8s03GOlTAdgZZ059w0fbqs4=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lAHORPa0Bs77zqhfzrB68kA"},"target":"bfc2ab1e9cecf2cbf60cd77b861912cade0d0f6998875a631a6d8153d8b4e274","message":"# Migrating from JAX-FEM to FEAX\n\nWe want to evaluate migrating the jNO FEM backend from **JAX-FEM** to **FEAX**.\n\nThe motivation is mainly:\n\n- cleaner integration with the JAX-native traced workflow in jNO,\n- fewer limitations in the current JAX-FEM route,\n- and better support for time-dependent FEM workflows moving forward.\n\nA key technical reason is that **JAX-FEM relies on PETSc for assembly**, so the assembly path is not fully inside JAX. This makes integration with JAX-native solver and optimization libraries harder, especially for workflows where we want the full FEM pipeline to remain differentiable and composable inside the traced setup. In particular, using JAX-based solvers such as **Optimistix** becomes more difficult in the current setup.\n\n**FEAX**, in contrast, is fully JAX-based and differentiable, which makes it a better fit for jNO's symbolic and compiled workflow. This could also open the door to more natural **solver-in-the-loop** and **fully differentiable simulation** workflows in the future. **FEAX** also supports time dependent FEM problems which can also be integrated into jNO.\n\nThis issue is meant to track the technical reasons for the migration, expected benefits, and compatibility checks for weak-form assembly and boundary handling.","files":null},{"type":6,"timestamp":1775652910,"nonce":"HSUKmR7/apN65uVMWVzxCr+HPg0=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lAHORPa0Bs77zqhfzrB7L00"},"target":"bfc2ab1e9cecf2cbf60cd77b861912cade0d0f6998875a631a6d8153d8b4e274","message":"# Migrating from JAX-FEM to FEAX\n\nWe want to evaluate migrating the jNO FEM backend from **[JAX-FEM](https://github.com/deepmodeling/jax-fem)** to **[FEAX](https://github.com/Naruki-Ichihara/feax)**.\n\nThe motivation is mainly:\n\n- cleaner integration with the JAX-native traced workflow in jNO,\n- fewer limitations in the current JAX-FEM route,\n- and better support for time-dependent FEM workflows moving forward.\n\nA key technical reason is that **JAX-FEM relies on PETSc for assembly**, so the assembly path is not fully inside JAX. This makes integration with JAX-native solver and optimization libraries harder, especially for workflows where we want the full FEM pipeline to remain differentiable and composable inside the traced setup. In particular, using JAX-based solvers such as **Optimistix** becomes more difficult in the current setup.\n\n**FEAX**, in contrast, is fully JAX-based and differentiable, which makes it a better fit for jNO's symbolic and compiled workflow. This could also open the door to more natural **solver-in-the-loop** and **fully differentiable simulation** workflows in the future. **FEAX** also supports time dependent FEM problems which can also be integrated into jNO.\n\nThis issue is meant to track the technical reasons for the migration, expected benefits, and compatibility checks for weak-form assembly and boundary handling.","files":null},{"type":5,"timestamp":1775652619,"nonce":"/FHZtkKIJxInveAuSZ5K6Z/rAl4=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADORPa0Bs77zqhfzwAAAAWoIc2h"},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1775652847,"nonce":"Evic9wjO0bW2ObWwV1Qgj3x+3sU=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDORPa0Bs76uBek","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/18#issuecomment-4206368676"},"message":"- Currently, the FEAX Migration is underway and all the features of jax-FEM has been incorporated with the FEAX integration.\n-  Time depenedence in FEAX is to be further examined \n     - Check [Difrax](https://github.com/patrick-kidger/diffrax) for Time dependent solvers.","files":null},{"type":6,"timestamp":1775652989,"nonce":"2R+JEEfWhiKbOIpiashQwL7+LSo=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lALORPa0Bs76uBekzqvcWmE"},"target":"9c7be47502c5fb51187f359684fc7a6261296c0d2259be5e8b2d89bc445514f2","message":"- Currently, the FEAX Migration is underway and all the features of Jax-FEM has been incorporated with the FEAX integration.\n     - FEAX has very simialr problem definition and strucutre as Jax-FEM, hence the migration is fairly direct.\n-  Time depenedence in FEAX is to be further examined \n     - Check [Difrax](https://github.com/patrick-kidger/diffrax) for Time dependent solvers.","files":null}]}